WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.050 --> 00:00:09.887
This slide introduces a landing zone as the governance foundation established before application teams deploy workloads.

2
00:00:09.887 --> 00:00:13.211
Begin with AWS Organizations in the middle.

3
00:00:13.211 --> 00:00:28.134
Its hierarchy separates a Security OU containing log archive and audit accounts, an infrastructure and shared services OU for networking, identity and reusable capabilities, and a Workloads OU dividing nonproduction from production.

4
00:00:28.134 --> 00:00:32.116
Follow the dotted arrow to AWS Control Tower on the right.

5
00:00:32.116 --> 00:00:44.436
Control Tower helps orchestrate an opinionated multi-account setup with identity integration, networking patterns, centralized logging, encryption standards, preventive policies, and account vending.

6
00:00:44.554 --> 00:00:49.775
These are baseline capabilities rather than automatic security for every application.

7
00:00:49.775 --> 00:00:59.182
At the bottom, the three benefits are consistent account security foundations, application team autonomy within guardrails, and standardized audit evidence.

8
00:00:59.182 --> 00:01:03.396
A landing zone isn't merely a VPC or an account structure.

9
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:10.071
It is the governed operating model for how new AWS accounts enter the organization and stay compliant.

10
00:01:16.217 --> 00:01:23.628
This diagram shows how AWS Organizations arranges its root, organizational units and member accounts.

11
00:01:23.628 --> 00:01:29.143
Start at the top with the root of the organization, and notice the management account beside it.

12
00:01:29.143 --> 00:01:36.076
That account retains organization administration and billing and is not restricted by service control policies.

13
00:01:36.076 --> 00:01:42.301
Follow the hierarchy downward to the Security OU, Workloads OU and Sandbox OU.

14
00:01:42.301 --> 00:01:45.819
Security contains log archive and audit accounts.

15
00:01:45.819 --> 00:01:52.777
Workloads branches into production and nonproduction OUs; each contains its own app or data accounts.

16
00:01:52.777 --> 00:01:56.540
The dashed boundaries illustrate inherited SCPs.

17
00:01:56.657 --> 00:02:04.944
The baseline attached to the root applies to member accounts, and the stricter production OU policy further restricts accounts beneath it.

18
00:02:04.944 --> 00:02:12.663
On the right, the slide emphasizes that SCPs set maximum permission boundaries and do not grant IAM permissions.

19
00:02:12.663 --> 00:02:22.019
An OU is only an organizational container, not a separately authenticated AWS account or an IAM Identity Center assignment target.

20
00:02:22.019 --> 00:02:27.870
When you move an account between OUs, review inherited guardrails before and after the move.

21
00:02:34.008 --> 00:02:40.208
This diagram separates ownership of the organization from supported delegated security administration.

22
00:02:40.208 --> 00:02:45.556
On the left, the AWS Organizations management account is the organization owner.

23
00:02:45.556 --> 00:02:51.755
It handles consolidated billing, policy administration, and the account and OU lifecycle.

24
00:02:51.755 --> 00:02:56.626
The tree underneath shows production, development and shared-services OUs.

25
00:02:56.626 --> 00:02:59.951
Follow the arrow toward the security account on the right.

26
00:02:59.951 --> 00:03:10.299
The management account can register a member account as delegated administrator for supported services such as Security Hub, GuardDuty, Firewall Manager and Inspector.

27
00:03:10.416 --> 00:03:20.779
That security account can operate service-level organization features within the allowed scope, reducing the need to perform routine security tasks from the management account.

28
00:03:20.779 --> 00:03:34.864
The three bottom decisions are to protect management-account access, delegate supported services where appropriate, and remember that delegation does not transfer ownership of Organizations, consolidated billing or general account moves.

29
00:03:34.864 --> 00:03:40.353
This distinction is central to designing a secure enterprise AWS account structure.

30
00:03:46.467 --> 00:03:52.770
The center of this architecture is an AWS Control Tower landing zone built on Organizations.

31
00:03:52.770 --> 00:03:55.708
Start there and examine the connected boxes.

32
00:03:55.708 --> 00:04:03.453
The security OU on the left contains a log archive account for centralized records and an audit account for security analysis.

33
00:04:03.453 --> 00:04:18.247
Below it, governance controls have different jobs: preventive controls block unwanted operations, detective controls report noncompliance, and supported proactive controls validate proposed CloudFormation resources before provisioning.

34
00:04:18.247 --> 00:04:23.363
At the top, IAM Identity Center provides workforce access to enrolled accounts.

35
00:04:23.475 --> 00:04:31.827
On the right, Account Factory processes requests, provisions new accounts and enrolls eligible accounts into governed OUs.

36
00:04:31.827 --> 00:04:39.971
Below it, organization-wide CloudTrail and AWS Config provide an operational audit and configuration baseline.

37
00:04:39.971 --> 00:04:48.708
The lower portion shows enrolled security, workload and sandbox accounts alongside compliance dashboards and central log analysis.

38
00:04:48.708 --> 00:05:00.035
Control Tower coordinates a repeatable governance foundation, but workload owners still must secure their own resources and confirm that required controls are supported in their regions.

39
00:05:06.175 --> 00:05:09.785
This slide follows an account through its governance lifecycle.

40
00:05:09.785 --> 00:05:12.130
Read the five steps from left to right.

41
00:05:12.130 --> 00:05:20.403
First, a business owner requests an account and identifies its environment, account email, intended OU and cost center.

42
00:05:20.403 --> 00:05:27.414
Second, the organization approves the business need, security and compliance requirements, and selected OU.

43
00:05:27.414 --> 00:05:38.058
Third, AWS Control Tower Account Factory provisions a new account or enrolls an eligible existing account, applying the supported baseline and guardrails.

44
00:05:38.058 --> 00:05:41.847
The OU selection determines which inherited SCPs apply.

45
00:05:41.959 --> 00:05:48.455
Fourth, IAM Identity Center assigns permission sets to users or groups for that specific account.

46
00:05:48.455 --> 00:05:52.540
Creating an account by itself does not create universal access.

47
00:05:52.540 --> 00:06:02.528
Fifth, the organization monitors logs, budgets, compliance and configuration drift, then formally moves or retires the account when appropriate.

48
00:06:02.528 --> 00:06:09.306
The lower panels show the account vending interfaces: Service Catalog and an optional Terraform approach.

49
00:06:09.306 --> 00:06:17.128
The overall decision is to automate the account lifecycle while retaining approval, access control and compliance review.
